    title: 'Lipid Metabolism and Transport',
    passageText: `Fatty acids are a major fuel source, particularly in prolonged fasting and exercise. Triglycerides (triacylglycerols) stored in adipose tissue are hydrolyzed by hormone-sensitive lipase (HSL), releasing free fatty acids (FFAs) into the bloodstream. FFAs travel bound to albumin to target tissues, where they undergo β-oxidation.

β-Oxidation occurs in the mitochondrial matrix. Long-chain fatty acids are activated to acyl-CoA in the cytoplasm, then transported across the inner mitochondrial membrane by the carnitine shuttle (carnitine acyltransferase I and II). Each round of β-oxidation cleaves off a 2-carbon acetyl-CoA unit, generating FADH₂ and NADH. Acetyl-CoA enters the TCA cycle; FADH₂ and NADH feed oxidative phosphorylation. A 16-carbon fatty acid (palmitate) generates 7 rounds of β-oxidation → 8 acetyl-CoA + 7 FADH₂ + 7 NADH.

Lipid transport in the bloodstream requires lipoprotein complexes because lipids are hydrophobic. Chylomicrons (from intestine) carry dietary lipids to peripheral tissues and the liver. VLDL (from liver) carries endogenous triglycerides to peripheral tissues. LDL (derived from VLDL) carries cholesterol to peripheral tissues; high LDL is associated with cardiovascular risk. HDL carries cholesterol from peripheral tissues back to the liver (reverse cholesterol transport); high HDL is cardioprotective.

Fatty acid synthesis occurs in the cytoplasm, requires NADPH (from the pentose phosphate pathway), and uses malonyl-CoA as the building block. The rate-limiting enzyme is ACC (acetyl-CoA carboxylase), which converts acetyl-CoA → malonyl-CoA. Fatty acid synthesis and β-oxidation are reciprocally regulated: when malonyl-CoA is high (active synthesis), it inhibits CPT-I (carnitine palmitoyl transferase I), preventing fatty acid entry into mitochondria and thus preventing futile cycling.`,
